# ADR-001: Implement JWT Refresh Token Rotation
# Status: Accepted
# Date: Week 2

## Context
CollabFlow currently issues 7-day JWTs with no rotation or
revocation capability (auth.ts line 42). If a token is stolen,
an attacker has 7 days of access with no ability to revoke it
short of changing the JWT signing secret (which invalidates ALL
users' sessions). At 800 DAU this is a manageable security risk;
at the CEO's 8,000 DAU target, the blast radius of a token theft
incident grows proportionally.

## Decision
Implement short-lived access tokens (15 minutes) with long-lived
refresh tokens (7 days) stored in the database and rotated on
each use. This gives users the same effective session duration
while providing token theft containment (max 15 min of unauthorized
access) and revocation capability (invalidate the refresh token).

## Options Considered
1. Refresh token rotation (chosen): strongest security, adds DB
   write on every token refresh (every 15 minutes per user)
2. Keep 7-day JWT, add revocation list in Redis: adds infrastructure
   (Redis) complexity; Module 37's premature complexity warning
   applies at current scale. Deferred.
3. Keep current (no change): unacceptable security posture as
   user base grows.

## Consequences
- Every 15 minutes, clients must refresh the access token
  (transparent to users with correct client implementation)
- Adds one DB write per refresh (negligible at 800 DAU)
- Enables user-by-user session revocation (admin capability)

## To Be Revisited If: refresh token DB writes become
## a measured performance concern (Module 36 will reveal this)

---

// IMPLEMENTATION SKETCH:
async function refreshAccessToken(refreshToken: string) {
  const stored = await db.findRefreshToken(refreshToken);
  if (!stored || stored.expiresAt < new Date()) {
    throw new UnauthorizedError('Invalid or expired refresh token');
  }

  // ROTATION: invalidate old token, issue new one
  await db.deleteRefreshToken(refreshToken);
  const newRefreshToken = generateSecureToken();
  const newAccessToken = generateJWT({ userId: stored.userId }, '15m');

  await db.saveRefreshToken({
    token: newRefreshToken,
    userId: stored.userId,
    expiresAt: addDays(new Date(), 7)
  });

  return { accessToken: newAccessToken, refreshToken: newRefreshToken };
}

