// CAPACITY ANALYSIS

// EC2 CPU:
// Current: 52% at 120 concurrent
// 2x (240 concurrent): 104% -> CRITICAL (>100% = FAILURE POINT)
// Intervention at 2x: EC2 autoscaling group (2 instances) + ALB
// Note: 2 instances -> 2 processes -> 24 connections to DB

// DB CPU:
// Current: 28% at 120 concurrent
// 2x: 56% (warning zone)
// 4x: 112% -> CRITICAL
// Intervention at 4x: load test + EXPLAIN first, then decide replicas

// DB CONNECTIONS:
// 1 instance: 12 connections (pool size 12)
// 2 instances: 24 connections
// 3 instances: 36 connections (monitor: PostgreSQL default max: 100)
// 5 instances: 60 connections (getting close, consider pgBouncer)

// Redis:
// Current: 128MB at 120 concurrent
// 10x: 1.28GB -> exceeds 512MB maxmemory -> CRITICAL at ~4x
// Intervention at 4x: increase Redis to 2GB or use Elasticache cluster

// WebSocket:
// Current: 120 connections, 1 instance
// 2x: 240 connections -> multi-instance ROUTING PROBLEM
// Required with first horizontal scaling: Redis Pub/Sub adapter

// BOTTLENECK SEQUENCE:
// 2x: EC2 CPU (with Redis adapter for WebSocket routing)
// 4x: DB CPU + Redis memory (measure first with load test)
// 8x: Connection pool exhaustion if >6 instances (add pgBouncer)
// 10x: Full Kubernetes evaluation (operational complexity justified)

// ADR FOR 2x INTERVENTION:
// # ADR-006: EC2 Autoscaling Group With Redis Socket.io Adapter
// ## Status: Accepted
// ## Context:
// EC2 CPU projected at 104% at 2x (240 concurrent users).
// Current single-instance can handle 120 concurrent before
// performance degrades. 120 * 2 = 240 exceeds 4-CPU capacity.
// Horizontal scaling creates the multi-instance WebSocket routing
// problem (users on Instance A won't receive messages from Instance B).
// ## Decision:
// Scale to 2 EC2 instances behind an ALB, simultaneously deploying
// the Redis Pub/Sub adapter to solve the WebSocket routing problem.
// ## Options Considered:
// 1. Vertical scale (c5.2xlarge, 8 CPU) - simpler, no WebSocket issue,
//    but prevents future horizontal scaling and costs 2x per instance
//    vs 2 c5.xlarge instances
// 2. Horizontal (chosen) - enables future scaling, familiar AWS tooling
// 3. Kubernetes - premature: team of 3 (2 junior) insufficient for
//    Kubernetes operational complexity at this traffic level
// ## Consequences:
// Redis becomes a higher-criticality dependency (single point of
// failure for both queues AND WebSocket routing). Add Redis HA
// evaluation to Month 3 roadmap.

